ke a hot-path scan over a collection whose volume nobody stated. One reviewer reads 'how often the path runs' as enough for a WARNING on its own. Another reads the unstated volume as what 'leaves that open', and files an observation. The change record's consumer note ('or the path plainly runs often') supports the first reading. The relational half ('makes it a cost someone meets') supports the second, because frequency over a small collection costs nothing. Instance: say which one wins in the bullet. One option is 'frequency alone warrants WARNING only where the collection plainly grows with use; otherwise an observation naming Access Paths'. If you change it, pin it with a clause test in TestReadCostToken. Accepting it as written is also reasonable: the check is capped at WARNING, and either reading names the shape and the measurement that settles it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "read-cost: Access Paths' per-entity 'Expected volume' sits beside the NFR template's Scalability 'data growth', and neither names the other", "recommendation": "Two homes now hold the stated volume. `plugin/templates/nonfunctional-requirements.md` Scalability already asks for 'expected user count and data growth over time'. The new Access Paths section asks for an order of magnitude per entity. The Critic bullet reads both ('stated volume (data model, NFRs)'), so a product that fills both can state two figures that disagree, and the reviewer has no rule for which one governs. The two differ in granularity (product-level vs per-entity), so this is not true duplication. Instance: have the Access Paths comment say its per-entity volumes refine NFR Scalability's figure. Alternatively, accept the overlap as granularity. No other template or doc lists data-model sections that would need the new heading: planning.md is the only enumerator, and it was updated.", "files": ["plugin/templates/data-model.md"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: Pipeline Coverage: Critic and artifact legs exist, discovery and builder legs are absent by recorded scope", "recommendation": "Framework Check 10 rates a missing dimension a warning at the boundary. Here the absence is deliberate and on the record. The owner's 2026-11-11 ruling scoped the work to three parts. The corrected Performance row says 'None' in the discovery and builder cells, and Known Gaps ties a revisit to the `read-cost:` yield. So the registry cross-check passes, because the row is accurate after the coverage change. As a result, Access Paths' only consumer is the Critic: no builder text asks the person writing a read to consult or update it. No edit is wanted beyond what Known Gaps already records. Generality (Check 7) passes: the check is stated as a property ('a read's cost should follow what its caller uses'), not as a list of database rules, and the template's 'key, index, partition or sort order' carries over to file, remote-API and embedded stores.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/templates/data-model.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: rated .prawduct/changes/read-cost-review.md, .prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md, .prawduct/owner-rulings.md (the whole oracle set); no bar cleared", "recommendation": "No edit wanted. I checked the claims the registry's Performance row and the change record make against the tree. discovery.md has 'Surface Operational Costs' and no volume or latency elicitation. building.md mentions performance only under the Optimization work type. templates/nonfunctional-requirements.md has Performance and Scalability sections. The stage scoping matches review-protocol.md (Goal 7, final/cumulative, observation at inner). The 'removal default' the change record cites is the proportionality norm in .prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md. None of this ships a false claim, misleads anyone into action, or must stop the merge.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/read-cost-review.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The test docstring's 'four different goals, never above note' figure rests on a scan whose derivation was thrown away", "recommendation": "The authoring rule applies here ('A spike that discards its code leaves its numbers unfalsifiable \u2014 commit the derivation as a runnable script and cite the COMMAND, never the digits'). TestReadCostToken's docstring, and the change record it echoes, state the result of a scan the change record calls throwaway. That scan found six instances in about four thousand reviews, under four goals. Nothing reads these figures, and the change record states its method and its sampling limit, so no edit is required. If this check's retirement is ever argued from its prior yield, that baseline needs a committed query; otherwise drop the figure from the docstring and keep it only in the dated change record.", "files": ["tests/test_control_yield_tokens.py"], "oid": "O-5"}], "duration_seconds": 300, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `feature/read-cost-review`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/read-cost-review.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/templates/data-model.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body